## Chapter 1.4: Spark into the Flame

Nadezhda and Luka walked side by side along the snow-covered street, white plumes of mist curling from their lips with every exhaled breath in the biting Siberian morning. Luka broke the silence first.

“So… what was that you synced earlier?” he asked, nodding toward the rolled sheet of e-paper tucked deep inside her winter coat pocket.

“Oh, this?” She pulled out the flexible cylinder and handed it to him. “Take a look for yourself.”

Luka unrolled the display and read through its contents. The heading read: *Vehicle Registration Dossier*, detailing exhaustive technical data about a _Russo-Balt[footnote:fhj2d] Burevestnik-GT/DVS_. Swiping across the surface brought up a full-resolution image: a muscular yellow sports car with an x-ray infographic showing its custom internal combustion engine, twin-scroll turbocharger, and bespoke mechanical linkages.

%Footnote.fhj2d: _Russo-Balt_ (Руссо-Балт [IPA_ru:ˈrusə bəlt]): One of the oldest and most prominent state automotive manufacturers in the Soviet Union. Operating out of the Baltic industrial corridor, the enterprise produces an all-around catalog of vehicles, ranging from reliable daily-commute family transports to specialized, high-performance grand tourers.

Luka had never been a car guy; electric municipal trams, high-speed rail, or a traditional walk through the snow were all he had ever needed. Still, the sleek aerodynamic profile and sheer internal complexity of the vehicle were undeniably captivating. Handing the e-paper back to her, he said, “Wow… Igor really didn’t hold back, huh?”

“Yup. That thing was definitely more expensive than the entire first floor of our station,” she laughed. “To be honest, I don’t mind someone having an eccentric hobby, but anything as extravagant as this is always an orange flag.” She rolled the e-paper back up and tucked it securely into her coat pocket.

The scrapyard was still a few blocks away, so Luka ventured another, slightly more casual question. “So, what’s your hobby, Officer Morozova? You mentioned an orange flag; should I assume your own hobby is also quite expensive?” He grinned, playfully calling back to her own words.

“Not nearly as expensive as this, of course… But I have a side project,” she hesitated for a moment, then decided to share anyway. “A channel on Spark[footnote:f6m5z].” 

%Footnote.f6m5z: _Spark_: An international, decentralized open-source crowdfunding and video-sharing platform for independent makers and hobbyist innovators. In a post-scarcity society where basic necessities are universally guaranteed, citizens voluntarily pool surplus energy credits to fund eccentric, artistic, or unconventional passion projects that fall outside formal state-planned research grants.

Luka’s eyes lit up. He hadn’t expected an officer of the law to be an active innovator and content creator on Spark. “Oh! That’s great, actually—Alex is on it too. How many followers do you have?” he asked eagerly, a genuine spark of connection forming between them.

“I only started about a year ago, so just a couple thousand. I don’t upload that often anyway—mostly just support other people's projects,” she replied humbly.

“Let’s exchange channels. I’ll send you Alex’s too,” he offered, pulling out his phone, opening the Spark app, and navigating to Alex’s profile via Touchy[footnote:f6fc1].

%Footnote.f6fc1: _Touchy_: A short-range, encrypted peer-to-peer data exchange protocol built into personal electronic devices. Requiring physical proximity within 10 centimeters, users initiate an instant transfer by lightly tapping their devices together—commonly used to exchange contact cards, application profiles, and creator handles without routing through public networks.

Nadezhda brought out her own phone, opened her creator page, and tapped the edge of her casing lightly against Luka’s. Within a second, their respective channels popped up on each other's screens. 

“Elektrifinadtsiya…[footnote:f8ls9]” Luka tried sounding out the syllables slowly, wrestling with the unfamiliar rhythm until the linguistic pun clicked in his head. He chuckled warmly. “Ah, clever wordplay! So you do electronic tinkering?” he asked, reading her channel tagline: *“Elektrifinadtsiya, the concept of pushing normal electronics to their absolute limit just for the bit.”*

%Footnote.f8ls9: _Elektrifinadtsiya_ (Электрифинадция [IPA_ru:ɪlʲɪktrʲɪfʲɪˈnatsɨjə]): A portmanteau blending _Elektrifikatsiya_ (Электрификация: electrification) with _Nadya_. Localized in English as “Electrifi-Nadya-tion” (pronounced like “Electrifination,” with “na” sounding like “Nadya / nya”), the title playfully subverts the historic state industrialization slogan to describe her personal maker philosophy of pushing ordinary household appliances to their   absolute limits.

“Uh-huh. Not a new concept, to be frank, but it’s a lot of fun.” But as she glanced down at her own screen, her eyes went wide at the sheer follower count on Alex’s profile. “Whoa! 100k?! ‘Ob Dynamo[footnote:fhmlh]’?! Wait, I’ve had his videos recommended to me before! I never checked them out because the heavy-industrial stuff felt a bit out of my lane, but wow…” She scrolled down to skim the video grid, then suddenly paused. “Wait… is this you?” 

%Footnote.fhmlh: _Ob Dynamo_ (Обь-Динамо [IPA_ru:ˈopʲ dʲɪˈnamə]): The popular hardware engineering channel run by Aleksey Volkov. The name was coined on a whim during his university days in Novosibirsk, inspired by a glance out his student dormitory window at the frozen Ob River while repairing a salvaged electrical generator. The channel evolved from student garage experiments into a major open-source hub for heavy-duty kinetic hardware, high-voltage physics, and magnetic coil engineering.

Luka glanced at her phone. “Yeah. I feature in his videos sometimes. Usually, I’m his cameraman and chalkboard calculator,” he replied. A quiet, bittersweet pang tightened in his chest. Ever since he developed the visions three months ago, an unspoken wall of dread had lingered between him and Alex. Until this vision matter was resolved, their once-effortless friendship remained stuck in an anxious, stagnant limbo.

“I see. Well, we should definitely collab sometime,” she gave a dry, teasing laugh—half-serious, half-joking. 

They continued chatting comfortably as they walked, before she glanced over at him. “Now that you know this much about me, let’s drop the formality. Call me Nadya.”

Luka’s circle had always been confined to theoretical physicists and lab researchers. This was the first time he had ever made friends with someone who clearly wielded legal authority. Despite being around the same age, the badge and uniform still made it feel a bit awkward for him. Nonetheless, he had been invited to.

“I-I’m honored, Comr— sorry, Nadya,” he said, catching himself with a flustered smile.

“Look at you, making this all formal. Come on, can’t a Militsiya girl have friends?” she giggled. “So what should I call you—Lukasha, or just Luka?”

“Just Luka is fine. My colleagues also call me that,” he replied.

“Luka it is then.” She pulled her hand from her pocket and extended it toward him. “Nice to meet you, Luka.”

“Nice to meet you too,” Luka smiled, taking her hand in a warm, firm handshake.

After their wholesome interaction, they finally arrived at the district scrapyard, the crime scene. There were already so many people, citizens and Militsiya alike, gathered around the perimeter in the cold. The high-contrast red-and-white striped barrier tape was visible from afar, fluttering in the icy mountain wind. Multiple dark blue patrol cars and a single municipal fire truck were parked nearby in the snowbanks, even though the fire had already been put out.

Luka could sense the excitement in Nadya as every step she took grew faster and more eager. He adjusted his stride to match her pace until they reached the outer edge of the cordon.

The central yard came into full view. The first sight that caught their eyes was a dark, burnt sports car—or what was left of its metal frame. It seemed they arrived just in time to witness the body retrieval process; firefighters were carefully cutting through the warped metal door with heavy shears to access the charred body inside.

“Excuse me, make way for an officer, please,” Nadya muttered as she made her way through the murmuring crowd, with Luka trailing right behind her. Once she reached the fluttering barrier tape, she turned back with a mischievous smirk.

“Alright, Comrade Huo. This is where our ‘equality’ comes to an end…” she said playfully, knowing full well only she could cross into the scene.

Luka, catching what she implied, returned the deadpan humor. “Understood, Comrade Junior Lieutenant. I’ll be observing the situation with great interest from back here.”

Nadya nodded with a grin. Ducking under the striped tape, she crossed into the perimeter, leaving Luka behind among the crowd.

Luka glanced around. Where he was currently standing was the entrance; the weathered, rusty metal sign above the gate read: _Vtorchermet_[footnote:fdw5v]. Beyond that gate lay a sprawling open yard with towering heaps of scrap metal gathered close against the precast concrete walls—primarily scrap from factories, train tracks from the freight line, compacted car frames, and a few vacuum flanges he even recognized from PIRM. The wall opposite the entrance housed a two-story brick office building and a snow-covered truck garage.

%Footnote.fdw5v: _Vtorchermet_ (Вторчермет [IPA_ru:ftərtɕɪrˈmʲet]): A Soviet industrial portmanteau for _Vtorichnye Chyornye Metally_ (Вторичные чёрные металлы [IPA_ru:vtɐˈrʲitɕnɨjə ˈtɕɵrnɨjə mʲɪˈtalɨ]: Secondary Ferrous Metals). Operating regional processing depots across the union, the state enterprise is tasked with the collection, sorting, and recycling of heavy scrap iron, steel railway tracks, factory infrastructure, and decommissioned vehicle frames.

The star of the show, the sports car, was neatly positioned in the very middle of the yard, away from other piles of metal. It was unclear whether this was a way to intimidate the public or if the perpetrator simply didn’t want to also be labeled an arsonist.

It seemed that by the time they arrived, the only thing left to be investigated was the body, as other yellow number tents were already scattered around the area. The Militsiya officers gathered and watched from a safe distance, away from the firefighters at work.

Across the snow, Luka saw Nadya talking with a senior officer. He couldn’t hear what they were saying because of the gossiping crowd and the loud metal-cutting noise, but the senior officer clearly didn’t look very happy seeing her here.

After observing everything, he decided he had seen enough. He had no interest in stealing investigative duties from the officers. Just as he was about to pull out his phone, a finger tapped on his shoulder. 

He turned around.

“Little[footnote:f9vcz] Huo! Didn’t expect to see you here! Come to see the talk of the town?” 

A woman stood in front of him, speaking in Dongbei[footnote:fdzq1] Chinese dialect. She wore round prescription glasses and a stylish winter coat with fur around the collar; on its left chest was the printed symbol of the _Heilongjiang People’s Daily_[footnote:fmzbs]. 

%Footnote.f9vcz: _Little_ (小 / _Xiǎo_ [IPA_zh:ɕjɑʊ̯]): In Chinese naming culture, the prefix _Xiǎo_ (Little) is placed before a surname or given name as a warm, affectionate diminutive. Commonly used among close friends, neighbors, and senior colleagues when addressing someone younger, it denotes fond familiarity rather than physical stature.

%Footnote.fdzq1: _Dongbei_ (东北 / _Dōngběi_ [IPA_zh:tʊ́ŋ.pèi]): The geographical and cultural region of Northeastern China (encompassing Heilongjiang, Jilin, and Liaoning provinces), directly bordering the Russian Far East. Known for its harsh sub-zero winters, heavy industrial heritage, and rich Sino-Russian border history, the regional Dongbei dialect is famous for its lively cadence, expressive humor, and warm, direct conversational style.

%Footnote.fmzbs: _Heilongjiang People’s Daily_ (黑龙江日报 / _Hēilóngjiāng Rìbào_ [IPA_zh:xeɪ.lʊ̌ŋ.tɕjɑ́ŋ ɻi.pɑ̂ʊ]): The official state newspaper of Heilongjiang Province, headquartered in Harbin. In the integrated Sino-Soviet Far Eastern corridor, the publication maintains active regional bureaus in border towns like Pogranichny, covering cross-border logistics, civic news, and scientific advancements from institutions like PIRM.

“Ah, Sis[footnote:f7f8c], I didn’t expect to see you here either! Not because you’re not a reporter, but because you hadn’t crossed my mind at all,” Luka sarcastically joked back in Dongbei. Aside from Alex, Lin Chen was another close friend of his. They had first met at a science symposium at PIRM about a month after Luka moved here. She had been the representative reporter for the People’s Daily in Pogranichny for almost six years.

%Footnote.f7f8c: _Sis_ (姐 / _Jiě_ [IPA_zh:tɕjɛ̀]): In Chinese social culture, addressing an older female friend, colleague, or mentor as _Jiě_ (Elder Sister / Sis) is a common honorific that conveys warm affection, mutual respect, and close camaraderie without the stiffness of formal workplace titles.

“Ouch… What did I do to be forgotten by Little Huo? Maybe I'll stop reporting on your work next time~” she jested back, knowing he didn’t mean it.

“Speaking of which, I didn’t see you in Vladivostok last Sunday. Did you forget to come, or…?” Luka asked, calling back to the WSS jubilee where he was a guest speaker.

“Oh, no, no, I didn’t forget. It’s just that over there is outside my coverage. Unlike you, I always keep you in mind, I’ll have you know,” she raised an eyebrow, a quick comeback to his earlier joke. “Also, if I’m not mistaken, there was a middle-aged guy representing the People’s Daily there, right? He graduated from Tsinghua[footnote:fdktc] too—no joke with that one.”

%Footnote.fdktc: _Tsinghua University_ (清华大学 / _Qīnghuá Dàxué_ [IPA_zh:tɕʰiŋ.xwa ta.ɕɥe]): Located in Beijing, Tsinghua is widely regarded as one of the most prestigious academic institutions in the world, globally renowned for its elite engineering, advanced physics, and rigorous scientific research standards.

Looking back, there certainly was a serious-looking reporter who had asked him several difficult questions; that must have been him. Compared to Lin, his questions were much more technical, while hers were more casual and approachable.

“Oh yeah, that guy was tough. Felt like talking with Dr. Krotov. During his panel, they also had quite a lot of… _discussion_,” he made an air-quote. He was glad the heat wasn’t on him when his turn came up. “But they seemed to get along quite well backstage though.”

“Heh, starting to miss me now after dealing with that guy?” she teasingly asked.

“Totally,” he admitted with a laugh and nodded.

“Anyway, how long have you been here?” he asked.

“Since six. I’m just waiting for them to get the body out,” she replied with a small sigh. “This is the first time in my life I’ve seen a real corpse, but at the same time, this is probably my biggest scoop yet. I can’t just walk away from it…” 

She continued, “He was corrupt and all that, but I don’t think anyone deserves this…” 

Her words led Luka to think about the implications too. Was Igor burned alive in this car? Being trapped in your own beloved possession while being slowly engulfed by flames was a horrifying thought. Or perhaps, in a better scenario, maybe he was already dead or unconscious? Even so, having your corpse desecrated and burned to a crisp, with no recognizable identity left, was brutal. Whatever happened, the perpetrator must have really despised Igor.

“I agree. He should have been brought to justice, not vigilantism,” Luka concurred.

Lin nodded in agreement. Then she leaned to the side to see past Luka. “Ah, look, they’ve already got the seat out,” Lin pointed out.

Luka turned back. The firefighters were carefully carrying out the charred body, which had melted into the seat, placing it in the cleared space. The crowd gasped, some in disgust, some in horror. People started whispering nervously to one another.

“Alright, I have to go take photos. Talk to you later, Little Huo!” She patted him on the back and made her way through the crowd to find a good angle.

He glanced around the yard again. The firefighters and Militsiya were discussing how to separate the body from the seat without damaging the body more than it already was. 

What was on Luka's mind, though, was a morbid curiosity and a collection of strange feelings. Since developing his vision, he had only ever used it on Alex and Nadya. But… what about Igor? Of course, the man was dead, but his theoretical mind couldn't stop nagging him.

_Would seeing into his future give me a clue about who did this?_ he thought. It was wishful thinking—what could looking into the future tell him about what happened in the past?

Yet all rationality had already been tossed aside. Being a theorist was all about exploring ridiculous notions. Pure human curiosity took over as he stared intently at the corpse and let his vision melt away…
